T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 1:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: State Coulomb's Laws of Dry Friction and explain the physical distinction between static friction, limiting friction, and kinetic friction.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Physical Nature and Mechanism of Dry Friction:) Tj
T*
(   Friction is a tangential contact force that opposes impending or ongoing relative sliding) Tj
T*
(   motion between two contacting surfaces. At the microscopic level, all engineering surfaces) Tj
T*
(   possess surface roughness with microscopic peaks \(asperities\). When two dry bodies are pressed) Tj
T*
(   together under normal compressive force N, true microscopic contact occurs only at asperity) Tj
T*
(   peaks,) Tj
T*
(   forming cold-welded junctions and mechanical interlocking that resist tangential shear.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Coulomb's Classical Laws of Dry Friction:) Tj
T*
(   - Law 1: The maximum static frictional force \(limiting friction F_lim\) is directly proportional) Tj
T*
(   to the magnitude of the normal contact reaction N: F_lim = mu_s * N.) Tj
T*
(   - Law 2: Limiting friction is independent of the apparent macroscopic contact surface area.) Tj
T*
(   - Law 3: Once macroscopic sliding commences, the kinetic friction force F_k is directly) Tj
T*
(   proportional to the normal reaction \(F_k = mu_k * N\) and is virtually independent of) Tj
T*
(   the relative velocity of sliding over moderate speed ranges.) Tj
T*
(   - Law 4: The frictional force depends fundamentally on the physical and metallurgical nature) Tj
T*
(   and surface condition/cleanliness of the contacting materials.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Three Distinct Regimes of Frictional Resistance:) Tj
T*
(   \(a\) Static Friction \(F_s\): A self-adjusting reaction force that balances the applied tangential) Tj
T*
(   shearing load F_app. As long as F_app < F_lim, the body remains in stationary equilibrium) Tj
T*
(   and F_s = F_app.) Tj
T*
(   \(b\) Limiting Friction \(F_lim\): The ultimate threshold of static resistance developed just as) Tj
T*
(   motion is on the verge of occurring \(impending slip\): F_lim = mu_s * N.) Tj
T*
(   \(c\) Kinetic \(Dynamic\) Friction \(F_k\): The resisting force during actual continuous sliding.) Tj
T*
(   Because moving asperities do not have sufficient contact time to fully deform and cold-weld,) Tj
T*
(   the kinetic coefficient is strictly smaller than the static coefficient: mu_k < mu_s.) Tj
T*
(   Consequently, F_k = mu_k * N < F_lim.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 2:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Define the Angle of Friction, Angle of Repose, and Cone of Friction, and prove that the Angle of Repose is identically equal to the Angle of Limiting Friction.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Definitions of Key Friction Parameters:) Tj
T*
(   \(a\) Angle of Friction \(phi_s\): The angle that the resultant contact reaction vector R makes) Tj
T*
(   with the normal reaction vector N when the body is in limiting static equilibrium \(impending) Tj
T*
(   slip\).) Tj
T*
(   Let F_lim = mu_s * N be the limiting friction force. In the right-angled contact triangle:) Tj
T*
(   tan\(phi_s\) = F_lim / N = \(mu_s * N\) / N = mu_s) Tj
T*
(   Therefore: phi_s = atan\(mu_s\).) Tj
T*
(   \(b\) Angle of Repose \(theta_r\): The maximum inclination angle of a rough planar surface relative) Tj
T*
(   to the horizontal at which a granular or solid body rests in static equilibrium without sliding) Tj
T*
(   under the sole action of gravity.) Tj
T*
(   \(c\) Cone of Friction: The right circular geometric cone generated by revolving the resultant) Tj
T*
(   reaction) Tj
T*
(   vector R around the contact normal N as its central axis. The semi-vertical apex angle of the) Tj
T*
(   cone is identically equal to phi_s. If the applied force vector results in an R located) Tj
T*
ET

