(>> Solution to Question 5:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Derive the Capstan belt friction equation T1 / T2 = exp\(mu * theta\) for a flat belt wrapped around a fixed rough cylindrical drum.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Differential Element Definition & Geometry:) Tj
T*
(   Consider a flat belt wrapped around a stationary cylindrical drum of radius R.) Tj
T*
(   Total lap angle of contact is theta \(in radians\). Let T1 be the tight side tension) Tj
T*
(   and T2 be the slack side tension \(with impending slip towards the tight side, T1 > T2\).) Tj
T*
(   Isolate an infinitesimal belt element subtending a small angle d_theta at the center.) Tj
T*
(   At one end of the element, tension is T; at the other end, tension increases to T + dT.) Tj
T*
(   Normal reaction exerted by drum on element is dN; friction force opposing slip is dF = mu * dN.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Resolution of Forces on the Differential Element:) Tj
T*
(   \(a\) Radial Normal Equilibrium \(Sigma F_radial = 0, inward +\):) Tj
T*
(   dN - T * sin\(d_theta / 2\) - \(T + dT\) * sin\(d_theta / 2\) = 0) Tj
T*
(   For small angles: sin\(d_theta / 2\) =~ d_theta / 2.) Tj
T*
(   dN = T * \(d_theta / 2\) + \(T + dT\) * \(d_theta / 2\) = T * d_theta + 0.5 * dT * d_theta) Tj
T*
(   Neglecting higher-order differential product \(dT * d_theta\):) Tj
T*
(   dN = T * d_theta.) Tj
T*
(   \(b\) Tangential Equilibrium \(Sigma F_tangential = 0, along tight side +\):) Tj
T*
(   \(T + dT\) * cos\(d_theta / 2\) - T * cos\(d_theta / 2\) - dF = 0) Tj
T*
(   For small angles: cos\(d_theta / 2\) =~ 1.) Tj
T*
(   \(T + dT\) - T - dF = 0 => dT = dF.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Integration along Total Contact Angle:) Tj
T*
(   By Coulomb's friction law: dF = mu * dN.) Tj
T*
(   Substitute dN = T * d_theta:) Tj
T*
(   dT = mu * \(T * d_theta\)) Tj
T*
(   Separating variables:) Tj
T*
(   dT / T = mu * d_theta) Tj
T*
(   Integrating both sides from the slack side \(T = T2 at angle 0\) to tight side \(T = T1 at angle) Tj
T*
(   theta\):) Tj
T*
(   Integral_T2^T1 \(dT / T\) = Integral_0^theta \(mu d_theta\)) Tj
T*
(   ln\(T1 / T2\) = mu * theta) Tj
T*
(   Taking exponents on both sides:) Tj
T*
(   T1 / T2 = exp\(mu * theta\)) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Engineering Application:) Tj
T*
(   This exponential relation explains how capstans on civil construction sites and marine docks) Tj
T*
(   allow a small operator force T2 to hold huge cable tensions T1 with just 2 or 3 turns of rope.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 6:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: A wedge of angle 15 degrees is used to raise a vertical load of W = 20 kN guided vertically in smooth channels. The coefficient of friction at all contacting interfaces \(wedge-load, wedge-floor\) is mu = 0.25. Calculate the horizontal force P required to drive the wedge.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Problem Formulation and Angles of Friction:) Tj
T*
(   Vertical load: W = 20 kN guided vertically \(channel walls are smooth => friction on vertical) Tj
T*
(   guides = 0\).) Tj
T*
(   Wedge taper angle: alpha = 15 degrees.) Tj
T*
(   Coefficient of friction at all wedge surfaces: mu = 0.25.) Tj
T*
