(   [Problem Statement]: Explain the self-energizing and self-locking characteristics of drum brakes.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Problem Context & Governing Principles:) Tj
T*
(   This problem investigates the limiting equilibrium and friction mechanics for civil engineering) Tj
T*
(   applications.) Tj
T*
(   Governing criteria involve Coulomb's dry friction law, non-concurrent moment equilibrium,) Tj
T*
(   and normal/tangential coordinate projections at contacting interfaces.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Mathematical Formulation & Step-by-Step Analysis:) Tj
T*
(   Self-Energizing and Self-Locking Brakes:) Tj
T*
(   1. Leading Shoe \(Self-Energizing\): The friction force between drum and shoe produces a moment) Tj
T*
(   in the same direction as the actuating cylinder force, naturally pulling the shoe tighter) Tj
T*
(   against the drum.) Tj
T*
(   2. Trailing Shoe: Friction produces a moment opposing actuation, de-energizing the shoe.) Tj
T*
(   3. Self-Locking: If the frictional moment exceeds the restoring spring moment, the shoe locks) Tj
T*
(   onto the drum permanently, preventing rotation even without pedal effort.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Detailed Physical Verification & Equilibrium Check:) Tj
T*
(   - Normal contact forces must satisfy Sigma F_n = 0 across all boundary contact interfaces.) Tj
T*
(   - Frictional forces must not exceed limiting value F <= mu * N for stable static equilibrium.) Tj
T*
(   - Moment balance Sigma M = 0 ensures no rotational tipping or rocking instability occurs.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   4. Civil & Structural Engineering Implications:) Tj
T*
(   In foundation retaining walls, dam bases, and structural bolted connections, an adequate) Tj
T*
(   factor of safety \(FS >= 1.5\) against sliding is mandatory under severe seismic and wind loads.) Tj
T*
